{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XatJU5vXdX61FbqsZqY7cW","type":"tool_result","content":"760\t\n761\t\n762\tdef copy_live(root, *, seed=False, approves=None):\n763\t    \"\"\"Mirror the registries an act AUTHORISES into `docs/archive/last_approved/`;\n764\t    the sorted list of repo-relative paths written.\n765\t\n766\t    SCOPED TO THE ACT SINCE WI-571. The seed copies the whole tree once; a\n767\t    refresh copies ONLY the registry a `Status` move happened in plus every\n768\t    registry `approves` names (`_authorised_registries`), and leaves every other\n769\t    registry byte-identical to what it already was. The whole-tree copy this\n770\t    replaced re-sealed whatever off-spine drift was live at the moment of a\n771\t    spine-only approval, silently zeroing the off-spine census the snapshot is\n772\t    the only basis for. An untouched file is not \"written\", so both mirror rules\n773\t    stay satisfied without touching it.\n774\t\n775\t    Byte-for-byte (`shutil.copyfile`), the LIVE carrier only — and for a registry\n776\t    it copies, any OTHER-carrier file for the same stem is DELETED in the same\n777\t    act, or `spine_carrier.resolve` raises \"exists under BOTH carriers\" on the\n778\t    very next read of the snapshot. A copied registry with no live carrier is\n779\t    skipped and its stale snapshot copies removed; a registry OUTSIDE the act's\n780\t    scope is not touched at all.\n781\t\n782\t    **REFUSES to CREATE the directory unless `seed=True`.** That refusal is the\n783\t    bootstrap guard: the FIRST snapshot blesses whatever text it copies, so it\n784\t    must ride the owner's own reviewed signing commit and nothing else.\n785\t    `--seed` is reachable only from `intake.py snapshot --seed`, and\n786\t    `tests/test_baseline_snapshot.py` pins that no loop module, hook or\n787\t    `check.py` contains the flag.\n788\t\n789\t    **AND REFUSES TO REFRESH ONE WITHOUT AUTHORITY** (`refresh_refusal`, 2026-08-20).\n790\t    Creating was guarded and rewriting was not, which made the second act the\n791\t    cheap one: after the first signing this function re-blessed any text it was\n792\t    pointed at. A refresh that would absorb APPROVED text into a registry now\n793\t    needs either a `Status` flip in that registry or an `approves` ref naming it,\n794\t    which is recorded into the snapshot's prose stamp. Traced-cell refreshes are\n795\t    unaffected and need no flag.\n796\t\n797\t    **NOTHING SHOULD EVER WIRE THIS INTO A FRESHNESS STEP.** Exactly two callers\n798\t    are sanctioned: `intake._apply_flips` (the mechanical path, called AFTER the\n799\t    status write so the copy captures the flip — which is what makes the\n800\t    unanchored rule decidable) and `intake.py snapshot` (the human path). Not\n801\t    `agent_loop`, not `dispatch`, not the hooks, not `check.py`. A step that\n802\t    REGENERATED the snapshot would defeat the whole mechanism: the snapshot is\n803\t    deliberately behind live whenever an amendment is pending, and that lag IS\n804\t    the signal.\"\"\"\n805\t    base = snapshot_root(root)\n806\t    if not base.is_dir():\n807\t        if not seed:\n808\t            raise SystemExit(\n809\t                \"baseline_snapshot: REFUSED — {} does not exist, and creating it \"\n810\t                \"would bless whatever text happens to be in the tree right now. \"\n811\t                \"The first snapshot rides the owner's signing commit: run \"\n812\t                \"`intake.py snapshot --seed` there, after every pending row has \"\n813\t                \"been ruled.\".format(base)\n814\t            )\n815\t        base.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n816\t        to_copy = set(SNAPSHOTTED)  # the seed blesses the whole tree, once\n817\t    else:\n818\t        # The authority gate and the scope decision, keyed on the directory\n819\t        # EXISTING rather than on `seed`: creating is seeding and rewriting is\n820\t        # refreshing, whatever flag the caller passed. `--seed` against a\n821\t        # standing record is a mistake, and a mistake is exactly the thing that\n822\t        # must not sail past the check. `_refresh_targets` raises on refusal.\n823\t        to_copy = _refresh_targets(root, approves, seed, base)\n824\t    written = []\n825\t    copied_rels = []\n826\t    for rel in SNAPSHOTTED:\n827\t        if rel not in to_copy:\n828\t            continue\n829\t        suffixes = (\n830\t            spine_carrier.NEED_CARRIERS if rel == NEEDS_REL else spine_carrier.CARRIERS\n831\t        )\n832\t        live = spine_carrier.resolve(Path(root) / rel, suffixes)\n833\t        dest_dir = base / Path(rel).parent\n834\t        # Every carrier path for this stem, so the STALE one is removed whether\n835\t        # or not a live file is being written over it. Done before the copy, so\n836\t        # a carrier change lands as delete-then-write rather than leaving both.\n837\t        for cand in spine_carrier.carriers(rel, suffixes):\n838\t            stale = base / cand\n839\t            if stale.is_file() and (live is None or stale.name != live.name):\n840\t                stale.unlink()\n841\t        if live is None:\n842\t            continue\n843\t        dest_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n844\t        dest = dest_dir / live.name\n845\t        shutil.copyfile(live, dest)\n846\t        written.append(dest.relative_to(Path(root)).as_posix())\n847\t        copied_rels.append(rel)\n848\t    written = sorted(written)\n849\t    if approves:\n850\t        _record_approval(base, approves, copied_rels)\n851\t    return written\n852\t\n853\t\n854\tdef is_drifted(rel, id_col, live_row, snapshot_rows):\n855\t    \"\"\"True when this row claims approval-or-above AND its APPROVED cells differ\n856\t    from its copy in the snapshot.\n857\t\n858\t    A row BELOW approval is never drifted — it has made no claim to fall from,\n859\t
